---
title: Postrender
summary: Kit's prerender, at request time. A page whose render read nothing personal is stored whole on its first request, and later visitors get the stored bytes until an invalidation clears them.
related:
  - regions/server-islands
  - data-state/page-data
  - app/experiments
---

Prerender renders a page at build time. **Postrender** renders it at request time: the first
request renders the page as usual, and if that render read nothing personal, the whole response is
stored (HTML, headers, permanent redirects). Every later request for that URL gets the stored bytes.
Kit, your loads and Svelte never run on a hit. An invalidation clears the page, and the next request
renders it again, one time.

So the cost of a page moves from one render per request to one render per change. Prerendering is the
special case where the only change is a deploy.

Postrender is its own package. Install it, list its plugin beside `ogygia()`, and put its handle
before ogygia's:

```bash
pnpm add @ogygia/postrender
```

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { postrender } from '@ogygia/postrender/vite';

plugins: [ogygia(), postrender(), sveltekit()]
```

```ts title=src/hooks.server.ts
import { sequence } from '@sveltejs/kit/hooks';
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';
import { postrender } from '@ogygia/postrender';

export const handle = sequence(postrender.handle(), ogygia.handle());
```

That is the full setup. Pages are kept in an in-process memory store, with a 24-hour TTL backstop
(`postrender({ ttl })` in seconds, to shorten it). Stampedes single-flight: fifty concurrent cold
requests make exactly one render. See [Plugins](/docs/reference/plugins).

`postrender.handle()` must come **before** `ogygia.handle()` in the `sequence()`. The stored page is
served before anything renders, so the store has to be known by then.

- In the wrong order, dev answers every page with an error that names the fix, and production warns
  once (it still serves from your store).
- Without `postrender.handle()` at all, pages go to the memory store and the server warns once, with
  the line to paste.

## Whole app, or opt in

By default (`postrender()`) the whole app is on. Each eligible page is stored, and the purity check
below refuses the rest, so a personalized page opts itself out. One route opts out in Kit's own
`config` page option:

```ts title=src/routes/account/+page.ts
export const config = { postrender: false };
```

To flip the whole app to **opt in**, so that nothing is stored unless a route asks, set the default
off:

```ts title=vite.config.ts
plugins: [ogygia(), postrender({ default: false }), sveltekit()]
```

Then a page or a layout opts in with `config = { postrender: true }`. Kit merges `config` from each
layout into the pages below it, and the page's own key wins. So a layout opts in its whole subtree,
and a page below can override in either direction:

```ts title=src/routes/(marketing)/+layout.server.ts
// this whole marketing subtree is stored
export const config = { postrender: true };
```

```ts title=src/routes/(marketing)/preview/+page.server.ts
// …except this one preview page, which stays per-request
export const config = { postrender: false };
```

`config` works in `+page.ts`, `+page.server.ts`, `+layout.ts` and `+layout.server.ts`, the same files
Kit reads it from. Other keys in the object (an adapter's `runtime`, for example) stay Kit's. The
value is read at build time, so write `true` or `false` directly: a computed value is a build error.
Opting a route in only makes it **eligible**. An impure render is still refused by the purity check
below.

### Programmatic routes

A table from the [server router](/docs/app/server-router) takes the same `config`, in its own
grammar, and merges it the same way: the table, then each layout, then the page.

```ts title=src/hooks.server.ts
const marketing = layout('marketing', Shell, { config: { postrender: true } });

export const app = routes(
  {
    ...marketing({
      '/':        page(Home),                                        // stored, from the layout
      '/preview': page(Preview, { config: { postrender: false } })   // per-request again
    }),
    '/account': page(Account)                                        // follows the vite-config default
  },
  { config: { postrender: false } }                                  // optional table-wide config
);

export const handle = sequence(postrender.handle(), ogygia.handle(), app.handle);
```

The router answers inside ogygia's handle, so a stored page is served before the table ever runs. The
router normally stamps its documents `cache-control: no-store`. On an eligible request it leaves that
to the verdict instead: a stored page gets `public, s-maxage`, a refused one gets `private, no-store`,
and a header your page sets itself always wins.

## What gets stored: observed purity, not annotations

Postrender watches each eligible render. A page **stays per-request** when the render:

- reads a cookie, personal header, or `locals` value that returned a **non-default** value,
- reads a [feature flag](/docs/app/experiments) (an A/B'd page must never store one variant),
- writes a cookie, sets `set-cookie`, answers non-200, or streams load promises,
- or was requested with a query string.

Each refusal is **named in a dev note** (`cookie:session`, `locals.user`, `flag:hero`). So an
almost-pure page tells you exactly which read keeps it per-request. Default-valued reads still store.
The render of an anonymous visitor (`cookies.get('consent')` returns `undefined`, an empty
`locals.user`) **is** the canonical copy. That is the cookie-less CDN cache key, formalized. Once
stored, all visitors receive the canonical. Content that must truly differ per visitor belongs in a
`render: 'deferred'` hole. The shell is stored. The hole fetches fresh per visitor. Its signed URL is
minted prerender-grade, so a warm stored page never carries expired holes (set a stable
`OGYGIA_SECRET`).

## Invalidation: three grammars

```ts title=src/routes/api/publish/+server.ts (or any server module)
import { postrender } from '@ogygia/postrender';

await postrender.invalidate('/fr/fr/solar/');             // exact URL — the CMS-webhook case
await postrender.invalidateWhere({ prefix: '/fr/fr/' });  // subtree — a locale or section nuke
await postrender.invalidate(loadDoc, ['promo']);          // declared source — see below
```

These reach the store and edges you gave `postrender.handle()`, with nothing else to set up. A
successful form action also evicts its own page automatically. This mirrors the
actions-invalidate-loads semantics of Kit.

**Declared sources** solve one-document-on-many-pages. Wrap the definition. Loads stay plain:

```ts title=src/lib/server/cms.ts
export const loadDoc = import.meta.og.source(async (slug: string) => fetchDoc(slug));
```

Each stored page whose render called `loadDoc('promo')` files a receipt.
`postrender.invalidate(loadDoc, ['promo'])` evicts exactly those pages, at the origin **and** at each
edge, however many there are, in whatever locale they live. The macro follows the
[macro laws](/docs/macros/overview): top-level `export const` only, AST-verified, loud build errors.
Pass `{ key: (…args) => string }` to canonicalize exotic argument shapes.

## Stores and edges

Live objects cannot ride vite config. They are the argument of `postrender.handle()`:

```ts title=src/hooks.server.ts
import { sequence } from '@sveltejs/kit/hooks';
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';
import { postrender, valkey, akamai, cloudfront } from '@ogygia/postrender';

export const handle = sequence(
  postrender.handle({
    store: valkey(client),                       // or upstash({ url, token }),
    edge: [akamai(creds), cloudfront(cfg)]       //    cloudflareKv(env.PAGES)
  }),
  ogygia.handle()
);
```

Stores: `memoryStore` (default) · `valkey(client)` (ioredis or node-redis) ·
`upstash({ url, token })` (REST, serverless-native) · `cloudflareKv(binding)` (Workers KV; the binding
comes from `import { env } from 'cloudflare:workers'`). Edges: `akamai` (EdgeGrid-signed Fast Purge) ·
`cloudfront` (SigV4 invalidations) · `cloudflare` (zone purge). Eligible responses are stamped
`cache-control: public, s-maxage=<ttl>`, plus the tags of each edge. So your CDN follows proven
per-page headers, not a blanket rule. An invalidation fans out to the store and each edge at the same
time. One edge that is down never fails a publish, or a request.

## Serve-time stitching

Sometimes even a one-beat canonical paint is too much: the first response itself must be personal.
Then mark the hole with `stitch`:

```svelte
<script>
  import Header from '$lib/Header.svelte' with { render: 'deferred', stitch: 'serve' };
</script>
```

The shell still stores one time, and serves as bytes. But on **each** serve, the server renders the
hole at that moment, with your cookies in hand, and splices it into the stored HTML before it
responds. One response, personalized in view-source, no JavaScript required. The trade is honest.
Stitched pages are per-visitor. So they are stamped `private, no-store` (no CDN copy, no 304s), and
a hit costs one region render, not zero. Failure is open: when the render of the hole fails or times
out, the fallback ships, and the browser fetches it exactly like a normal deferred hole.

### Edge stitching

`stitch: 'serve'` buys a personal first paint at the price of the edge copy. On a CDN that speaks
**ESI** (edge side includes: Akamai, Fastly, Varnish) you can keep both:

```svelte
<script>
  import Header from '$lib/Header.svelte' with { render: 'deferred', stitch: 'edge' };
</script>
```

The shell still stores one time, and it stays a normal edge-cached page: `public, s-maxage`,
validators and all. What changes is the hole. In the stored bytes it becomes an ESI include that
points at the hole's own signed capability, and the response carries
`Surrogate-Control: content="ESI/1.0"`. On each request the CDN serves the shell from its cache,
fetches only the include from your server with the visitor's cookies, and splices it at the edge.
The first response is personal, the shell never leaves the edge, and your server renders one region,
not a page. Enable ESI processing on the property for this to happen.

It degrades on its own. Anywhere ESI is not processed, direct to origin, in dev, or on a CDN without
it, the include is inert and the hole ships its fallback, then the browser fetches it exactly like a
normal deferred hole. One `stitch: 'serve'` hole on a page makes the whole page origin-stitched,
`edge` holes on it included.

Rule of thumb: `render: 'live'` when canonical-first is satisfactory. `stitch: 'edge'` when the first
paint must be personal and your CDN speaks ESI. `stitch: 'serve'` when it must be personal and it
does not.

## Notes

- **In dev, the store never serves or fills.** Your edits always render again. The verdict still
  teaches: an eligible page answers `x-ogygia-postrender: would-store` (check the network panel). A
  refused one prints a single console note that names the read that refused it.
- **Revalidations are free.** Stored entries carry an `etag` and `last-modified`. Postrender answers
  `If-None-Match` / `If-Modified-Since` with a bodiless 304. So a CDN prefresh, or a browser reload of
  a stored page, costs zero body bytes.
- **Refused pages get `private, no-store`** when your app set no cache-control of its own. The
  headers of the verdict replace blanket CDN rules, in both directions.
- **csr-agnostic**: a `csr = true` page stores the same way. Kit hydrates the stored copy.
- **The store holds the render**, not data. A hit skips the data fetch _and_ the component execution.
  Your data caches stay useful underneath, for the misses.
- Store failures never fail a request. A broken store means renders, not errors.
- Blind spot, the same contract as prerender: `Date.now()`/randomness inside a load is "true at
  render time".
